  Terras identity (PROVED): for ANY T-invariant s, s(2^e n + r) = s(3^a n + T^e(r)), a = number of
  odd steps among the first e steps of r.  So the 2-kernel of an invariant set is a set of
  subsequences n -> s(3^a n + t); the k=2 rows of the three basins coincide EXACTLY with the
  cumulative number of distinct pairs (a, T^e(r)): [1, 2, 4, 7, 12, 21, 38, 69, 127, 235, 438, 819, 1535, 2883, 5425, 10218, 19275]
  (every pair gives a different subsequence, for each basin, up to e = 16).  The statistic is
  type-level: it cannot tell the basins apart, and it is 1 for the Collatz basin (all ones).
  Reading: bounded rows are automatic; rows growing like k^e are consistent with
  non-automaticity (evidence only; automaticity of the 3n-1 basins is not decided here).

==============================================================================
MB3  The rotation obstruction: 3-smooth numbers satisfy both rotation-Mahler equations
==============================================================================
  s(2n) = s(n), s(3n) = s(n) on [1, 1000000] (exact); #S cap [1,1000000] = 142 (~ (ln N)^2/(2 ln2 ln3) = 125)
  numerically: max |F(z^2)-(F(z)+F(-z))/2|, |F(z^3)-mean_j F(w^j z)| over 8 points = 8.0e-16
  S is infinite with density 0, hence not eventually periodic, so F is irrational; its exponents
  n_k satisfy n_k/k -> infinity (counting function O(log^2 N)), so |z| = 1 is a natural boundary
  (Fabry-Faber gap theorem).  Uncountably many more: S_A = {2^a 3^b m : m in A} for any A of
  integers prime to 6.  => The Adamczewski-Bell / Schafke-Singer theorem has no analogue for
  Mahler equations with roots of unity; the Collatz (Berg-Meinardus) equation is of that type.

==============================================================================
MB4  SHEET at the level of series: the negative half of 3n+1 is the 3n-1 problem
==============================================================================
  for every m <= 100000: the T_+-orbit of -m ends in the cycle of -1, -5 or -17 exactly when the
  T_- orbit of m ends in the cycle of 1, 5 or 17 (direct iteration of T_+ on negatives).
  T_+ cycle through -17: [-17, -25, -37, -55, -82, -41, -61, -91, -136, -68, -34]
  So for a T_+-invariant S in Z\{0}: sum_{n in S, n>0} z^n solves E_{3,+1} and
  sum_{n in S, n<0} z^{-n} solves E_{3,-1}; the second equation has three natural-boundary
  0/1 solutions (the 3n-1 basins).  A proof that E_{3,+1} has only rational 0/1 solutions
  must therefore use what distinguishes b = +1 from b = -1 in
      h(z^3) - h(z^6) = z^{-b} (1/3) sum_j w^{bj} h(w^j z^2):
  the monomial z^{-b} and the sieved class n = -b mod 3, i.e. the sign.

