==============================================================================
HG1  Every odd n >= 3 has an infinite (never-ending) play
==============================================================================
  for all odd 3 <= n < 10^6: exactly one of 3n+1, 3n-1 is 2 mod 4 and it yields the odd
  successor (3n+-1)/2 > n.  A 10^4-move ascending play from 3 ends at a number with 1762 digits.
  => infinite plays exist from every odd n >= 3; unlike Hex, a 'complete play' need not
     have a winner, so Gale's Hex <=> Brouwer mechanism (every filled board has exactly one
     winner) has no analogue.  The prize question is determinacy of optimal play.

==============================================================================
HG2  P-positions exist (the mover can lose): capped retrograde for odd n < 200
==============================================================================
  retrograde to cap 262144 (43 rounds): among odd n < 200: 57 N, 43 P, 0 unresolved
  P-positions below 200: [7, 13, 23, 25, 29, 41, 45, 51, 57, 63, 65, 71, 73, 79, 81, 89, 91, 93, 99, 101, 103, 111, 113, 115, 125, 127, 129, 141, 143, 145, 157, 159, 161, 163, 165, 175, 177, 179, 181, 183, 185, 197, 199]
  7 is P: its moves go to 11 and 5, and both are N (5 -> 1 wins; 11 -> 32 -> 1 wins).
  So the player to move loses at about half the positions (repo: P-density about 0.48 below
  2^32); no strategy-stealing argument gives a first-player win, and none addresses draws.

